\tag{connes}
\tag{lean}
\tag{proof-engineering}
\title{Structure forgetting and equivalence reuse}
\lean/connes{Connes.IsProjectionSupremum,Connes.StarAlgEquiv.isNormal}

\p{\strong{Separate the abstract normality contract from its concrete proof.}
The predicate
\leanref/connes{Connes.IsProjectionSupremum}
takes its order relation as a parameter and asks for leastness only among
projection upper bounds. It therefore avoids both a built-in
multiplication-based order wrapper and the stronger ambient \code{IsLUB}
contract. The predicate
\leanref/connes{Connes.IsNormalStarAlgEquiv}
then says, using only the operations it mentions, that an equivalence and its
inverse preserve these projection suprema. Uniqueness is exposed by
\leanref/connes{Connes.IsProjectionSupremum.unique}; identity, inverse, and
composition laws culminate in
\leanref/connes{Connes.IsNormalStarAlgEquiv.trans}.}

\p{For the concrete operator subalgebras used by the factors,
\leanref/connes{Connes.IsProjectionSupremum.map_starAlgEquiv}
instantiates that parameter with the inherited operator order and derives
transport from the characterization #{p\leq q\iff pq=p} for projections. The theorem
\leanref/connes{Connes.StarAlgEquiv.isNormal}
packages the forward and inverse directions. The abstract predicates stay in
the core specification; the operator-order proof is isolated with the other
projection infrastructure and consumed by the spatial factor witness. The
projection-valued spectral interface
\leanref/connes{Connes.ProjectionValuedSpectralMeasure}
and supremum transport remain separate focused modules in that projection
layer, while their public declarations remain stable.}

\p{\strong{Forget structure after it has served its consumer.}
The Section 3 shear is packaged as an equivariant measure-preserving
homeomorphism because pullback must preserve continuous coefficients. Its
canonical projection to an equivariant measurable equivalence is what the full
#{L^\infty} crossed-product transport consumes.}

\p{This one-way forgetting records
both facts accurately: Zhou's factor argument does not need an additive map,
while the Lean density bridge legitimately uses the continuity proved in
\citet{Proposition 3.2 and Remark 3.3}{zhou2026icc}.}

\p{\strong{Prefer library equivalences to pointwise cancellation.}
Mathlib already supplies continuous pullback as a star-algebra equivalence, so
its surjectivity gives the reverse generator inclusion without a custom
involutivity proof. Likewise, the inverse law for conjugation by a linear
isometry equivalence replaces a pointwise operator calculation. These
replacements shorten the proof while exposing the actual mathematical reason:
both steps are transport along equivalences.}

\p{\strong{Keep expensive elaboration at the generator-transport seam.}
The two kernel-generator identities for the
\leanref/connes/as{first}{Connes.PaperGroupFactor.paperGroupFactorUnitaryOne_conj_inl}
and
\leanref/connes/as{second}{Connes.PaperGroupFactor.paperGroupFactorUnitaryTwo_conj_inl}
factors, together with the corresponding
\leanref/connes/as{first}{Connes.PaperGroupQuotient.paperGroupFactorUnitaryOne_conj_inr}
and
\leanref/connes/as{second}{Connes.PaperGroupQuotient.paperGroupFactorUnitaryTwo_conj_inr}
quotient-generator identities,
carry the regular generators through the composed Fubini and Fourier #{L^2}
equivalences. They are implementation-level generator checks used to realize
the Fourier and crossed-product identifications invoked, but not expanded, in
\citet{Proposition 3.4}{zhou2026icc}.}

\p{These proofs now name the inverse image of a test vector and use pointwise
action formulas before normalizing the operator expression. Only these four
declarations retain a larger elaboration budget. The localization is a
performance signal about nested equivalence normalization, not an additional
mathematical assumption.}
